# Evals

Measured evidence that {{PROJECT_NAME}} fires when it should, stays quiet when it should not,
and does what `SKILL.md` says once loaded. Runs with `claude plugin eval` (Claude Code 2.1.269
or later): each case is a `prompt.md` plus one or more graders, run several times with and
without the plugin so the report shows what the skill actually contributes.

```sh
claude plugin eval . --ablation none -j 4 --judge-model sonnet   # cheap trigger check
claude plugin eval . -j 4 --judge-model sonnet                    # with the no-plugin baseline
```

Two cases are seeded. Replace the placeholder prompts with things a person would really type,
never the skill's name, and add a case for every rule in `SKILL.md` that matters.

| Case | Passes when |
| --- | --- |
| `triggers-on-the-right-request/` | The skill was invoked and the reply meets `graders/criteria.md` |
| `ignores-an-unrelated-request/` | The skill was **not** invoked |

Notes that save time: a `tool_used: Skill` grader is reported but not scored in the default
two-arm mode (it can never pass without the plugin), so use `--ablation none` when the trigger
rate is the number you want. `Bash`, `Write`, and `Edit` are stripped from runs unless granted
with `--allow-tools`. Give cases enough `max_turns` for the skill to load and reply — three is
not enough.

Record each run's date, Claude Code version, and headline numbers in the changelog when the
result changes a decision.
